# Session

Signed session cookies and CSRF tokens.

A session cookie lives in the visitor's browser, where the visitor can edit it. The
only thing between "logged in as me" and "logged in as anyone" is a signature the
server can check and the visitor cannot forge, so every function here is written
around the FORGERY case rather than the happy one.

`examples/login` is a signed-in site built on this module without calling it. Its
`## Session` block declares the session's fields, and the compiler writes the calls:
`signSession` and `verifySession` around the cookie, `sessionField` to read a field
back, `csrfToken` behind `csrf()` in a form, and `csrfValid` before any request that
changes state. The Sessions section of `docs/lgx-stdlib.md` shows both halves: the
sentence a program writes and the functions it becomes.

## Note
`t` up to and including its `n`th character; `""` when `n` is not positive.
## To textUpTo (t: Text) and (n: Int) -> Text:
    Let mutable out be "".
    Let mutable i be 0.
    For each c in t:
        Set i to i + 1.
        If i is at most n:
            Set out to out + c.
    Return out.

## Note
`t` after its `n`th character; the whole of `t` when `n` is 0.
## To textFrom (t: Text) and (n: Int) -> Text:
    Let mutable out be "".
    Let mutable i be 0.
    For each c in t:
        Set i to i + 1.
        If i is greater than n:
            Set out to out + c.
    Return out.

## Note
The 1-based position of the LAST `|` in `t`, or 0 when there is none.

The last, not the first, and that choice is a security boundary rather than a
preference: a session value may legitimately contain `|`, and a parser that split
at the first one would read `signSession(s, "a|b")` = `"a|b|MAC"` as the value `"a"`
carrying the signature `"b|MAC"` — letting an attacker present a DIFFERENT value
with a signature that checks.
## To lastSeparator (t: Text) -> Int:
    Let mutable at be 0.
    Let mutable i be 0.
    For each c in t:
        Set i to i + 1.
        If c is equal to "|":
            Set at to i.
    Return at.

## Note
`msg` authenticated under `secret`, as lowercase hex.
## To macHex (secret: Text) and (msg: Text) -> Text:
    Let mac be hmacSha256(text_bytes(secret), text_bytes(msg)).
    Let mutable out be "".
    For each b in mac:
        Let hi be b // 16.
        Let lo be b - (hi * 16).
        Set out to out + chr(hexDigit(hi)) + chr(hexDigit(lo)).
    Return out.

## Note
Whether `a` and `b` are the same text, reading every byte of two texts of one length before it answers.

The visitor chooses the signature a server checks. Text equality stops at the first byte that differs,
so the time a forged signature takes to refuse would say how much of its front is right; this check
takes the same steps wherever the texts differ, and leaks only their lengths, which are public.
## To sameTextInConstantTime (a: Text) and (b: Text) -> Bool:
    Let x be text_bytes(a).
    Let y be text_bytes(b).
    If (length of x) is not equal to (length of y):
        Return false.
    Let mutable diff be 0.
    Repeat for i from 1 to length of x:
        Set diff to diff + ((item i of x) xor (item i of y)).
    Return diff is equal to 0.

## Note
`value`, followed by its signature — the cookie a server hands out.
## To signSession (secret: Text) and (value: Text) -> Text:
    Return value + "|" + macHex(secret, value).

## Note
The signature half of a cookie, for a caller that wants to inspect it.
## To sessionMac (cookie: Text) -> Text:
    Return textFrom(cookie, lastSeparator(cookie)).

## Note
The value a cookie carries, or `none` when its signature does not check.

Every malformed shape answers `none` rather than trapping: a cookie is
attacker-controlled input, and a server that crashes on a hand-edited one has been
handed a denial of service.
## To verifySession (secret: Text) and (cookie: Text) -> Option of Text:
    Let at be lastSeparator(cookie).
    If at is equal to 0:
        Return none.
    Let value be textUpTo(cookie, at - 1).
    Let mac be textFrom(cookie, at).
    If sameTextInConstantTime(mac, macHex(secret, value)) is equal to true:
        Return some(value).
    Return none.

## Note
A CSRF token BOUND to one session.

Bound, because a token any session's token satisfies protects nothing — the
attacker simply uses their own. The `|csrf` suffix keeps the token's key distinct
from the session cookie's, so neither can be replayed as the other.
## To csrfToken (secret: Text) and (session: Text) -> Text:
    Return macHex(secret + "|csrf", session).

## Note
Whether `token` is the CSRF token for this `session`.
## To csrfValid (secret: Text) and (session: Text) and (token: Text) -> Bool:
    Return sameTextInConstantTime(token, csrfToken(secret, session)).

## Note
The UTF-8 bytes of `t` as lowercase hexadecimal; `unhexText` is the way back.

A cookie value may not contain `;`, `,`, `"`, a space or a control character — `isCookieSafe`
enforces exactly that — and a session payload is arbitrary text a visitor typed. Hex has no
escaping rules to get wrong and no character outside `0-9a-f`, so the payload cannot break the
cookie and cannot smuggle a second one.

## To hexText (t: Text) -> Text:
    Return bytesToHex(text_bytes(t)).

## Note
The text a `hexText` came from. Invalid hex answers `""` rather than trapping, because a cookie is
attacker-controlled input.

## To unhexText (h: Text) -> Text:
    Return text_from_bytes(bytesFromHex(h)).

## Note
Field `i` of a session payload — counting from 1, as LOGOS indices do — or `""` when the payload
does not carry one.

Fields are joined with `.`, which is safe precisely because every field is hex — the separator
cannot occur inside a value, so there is nothing to escape and no ambiguity to resolve. A payload
with too few fields is what a visitor holding a cookie from an OLDER version of the program sends,
so it answers the empty value rather than failing: a session that gained a field must not lock out
everybody who was already signed in.

## To sessionField (payload: Text) and (i: Int) -> Text:
    Let parts be split(payload, ".").
    If i is less than 1:
        Return "".
    If i is greater than length of parts:
        Return "".
    Return unhexText(item i of parts).

## Note
Field `i` of a session payload as a whole number, or `0` when it is absent or not one.

## To sessionInt (payload: Text) and (i: Int) -> Int:
    Let t be sessionField(payload, i).
    If isIntText(t) is equal to false:
        Return 0.
    Return parseInt(t).

## Note
Field `i` of a session payload as a truth value. Anything that is not `true` is false, which is
the safe direction for a flag a visitor holds a cookie for.

## To sessionBool (payload: Text) and (i: Int) -> Bool:
    Return sessionField(payload, i) is equal to "true".

## Note
The secret every session cookie is signed with.

`LOGOS_SESSION_SECRET` sets it. With nothing set it is RANDOM, once, per process — so a program
that never configures one still cannot have its sessions forged, and the cost is that sessions do
not survive a restart. A fixed default would be the other way round: convenient, and forgeable on
every LOGOS server anybody ever deployed without reading this note.

## To sessionSecret () -> Text:
    Let mutable found be "".
    Inspect get("LOGOS_SESSION_SECRET"):
        When Some (s):
            Set found to s.
        When None:
            Set found to "".
    If found is equal to "":
        Return randomSecret().
    Return found.

## Note
A fresh unguessable secret — 8 random 31-bit numbers in hex.

## To randomSecret () -> Text:
    Let mutable out be "".
    Let mutable i be 0.
    While i is less than 8:
        Set out to out + toHexInt(randomInt(0, 2147483647)).
        Set i to i + 1.
    Return out.
